@subsection Mutable List Structure

The basic operations on pairs---@code{cons} and the @code{car} and
@code{cdr} selectors---can be used to construct list structure and to select
parts from list structure, but they are incapable of modifying list structure.
The same is true of the list operations we have used so far, such as
@code{append} and @code{vlist}, since these can be defined in terms of
@code{cons} and the two selectors.  To modify list structures we need new
operations.

The primitive mutators for pairs are @code{setCar} and
@code{setCdr}. @code{SetCar} is called on the pair to modify, and that
receiver must be a pair.  It modifies this pair, replacing the @code{car}
pointer by a pointer to the argument of
@code{setCar}.@footnote{@code{SetCar} and @code{setCdr} return @code{Unit},
which carries no information.  Like assignment, they should be used only for
their effect.}

@noindent
As an example, suppose that @code{x} is bound to the list @code{((a b) c d)}
and @code{y} to the list @code{(e f)} as illustrated in @ref{Figure 3.12}.
Evaluating the expression @code{x.setCar(y)} modifies the pair to which
@code{x} is bound, replacing its @code{car} by the value of @code{y}.  The
result of the operation is shown in @ref{Figure 3.13}.  The structure @code{x}
has been modified and would now be printed as @code{((e f) c d)}.  The pairs
representing the list @code{(a b)}, identified by the pointer that was
replaced, are now detached from the original structure.@footnote{We see from
this that mutation operations on lists can create ``garbage'' that is not part
of any accessible structure.  The JVM reclaims a detached chain once no live
reference can reach it; we will see in @ref{5.3.2} that Lisp
memory-management systems include a @newterm{garbage collector}, which
identifies and recycles the memory space used by unneeded pairs.}

@example kotlin
val x = cons(vlist(VSym("a"),
    VSym("b")),
        vlist(VSym("c"),
    VSym("d")))
val y = vlist(VSym("e"), VSym("f"))

x.setCar(y)

x
// => ((e f) c d)
@end example

