@noindent
@code{Cons} builds new list structure by creating new pairs, while
@code{setCar} and @code{setCdr} modify existing pairs.  Indeed, we could
implement @code{cons} in terms of the two mutators, together with a function
@code{getNewPair} that returns a new pair that is not part of any existing
list structure; in this runtime that role is played by constructing a fresh
@code{VPair} with two empty slots.  We obtain the new pair, set its
@code{car} and @code{cdr} pointers to the designated objects, and return the
new pair as the result of the @code{cons}.@footnote{Constructing that fresh
cell is one of the operations that must be implemented as part of the memory
management required by a Lisp implementation.  We will discuss this in
@ref{5.3.1}.}

@example kotlin
fun cons(x: Value, y: Value): VPair @{
    val newPair = VPair(VNil, VNil)
    newPair.setCar(x)
    newPair.setCdr(y)
    return newPair
@}
@end example

@quotation
@strong{@anchor{Exercise 3.12}Exercise 3.12:} The following function for
appending lists was introduced in @ref{2.2.1}:

@example kotlin
fun append(x: Datum, y: Datum): Datum =
    if (x !is PairCell) @{
        y
    @} else @{
        pair(x.first, append(x.second, y))
    @}
@end example

@code{Append} forms a new list by successively pairing the elements of
@code{x} onto @code{y}.  The function @code{appendBang} is similar to
@code{append}, but it is a mutator rather than a constructor.  It appends the
lists by splicing them together, modifying the final pair of @code{x} so that
its @code{second} field is now @code{y}.  (It is an error to call
@code{appendBang} with an empty @code{x}; the @code{PairCell} parameter type
refuses one before the program runs.)

@example kotlin
fun appendBang(x: PairCell, y: Datum): PairCell @{
    lastPair(x).second = y
    return x
@}
@end example

Here @code{lastPair} is a function that returns the last pair in its
argument:

@example kotlin
fun lastPair(x: PairCell): PairCell =
    if (x.second === Empty) x else lastPair(x.second as PairCell)
@end example

Consider the interaction, in which @code{z} and @code{w} both hold the four
symbols @code{a}, @code{b}, @code{c}, @code{d} in order:

@example kotlin
val x = datumList(Symbol("a"), Symbol("b")) as PairCell
val y = datumList(Symbol("c"), Symbol("d")) as PairCell
val z = append(x, y)

x.second
// ⟨response⟩

val w = appendBang(x, y)

w === x
// => true

x.second
// ⟨response⟩
@end example

What are the missing @code{⟨}@var{response}@code{⟩}s?  A pair prints in the
constructor form of @code{renderDatum}: a list holding only the symbol
@code{q}, for example, prints as
@code{PairCell(first=Symbol(name="q"), second=Empty)}.  Draw box-and-pointer
diagrams to explain your answer.
@end quotation

@quotation
@strong{@anchor{Exercise 3.13}Exercise 3.13:} Consider the following
@code{makeCycle} function, which uses the @code{lastPair} function defined
in @ref{Exercise 3.12}:

@example kotlin
fun makeCycle(x: PairCell): PairCell @{
    lastPair(x).second = x
    return x
@}
@end example

Draw a box-and-pointer diagram that shows the structure @code{z} created by

@example kotlin
val z = makeCycle(
    datumList(Symbol("a"), Symbol("b"), Symbol("c")) as PairCell,
)
@end example

What happens if we try to compute @code{lastPair(z)}?
@end quotation

@quotation
@strong{@anchor{Exercise 3.14}Exercise 3.14:} The following function is quite
useful, although obscure:

@example kotlin
fun mystery(x: PairCell): PairCell @{
    fun loop(x: Datum, y: Datum): PairCell =
        if (x !is PairCell) @{
            y as PairCell
        @} else @{
            val temp = x.second
            x.second = y
            loop(temp, x)
        @}
    return loop(x, Empty)
@}
@end example

@code{Loop} uses the ``temporary'' variable @code{temp} to hold the old value
of the @code{second} field of @code{x}, since the assignment on the next line
destroys it.  Explain what @code{mystery} does in general.  Suppose
@code{v} is defined by

@example kotlin
val v = datumList(
    Symbol("a"),
    Symbol("b"),
    Symbol("c"),
    Symbol("d"),
) as PairCell
@end example

Draw the box-and-pointer diagram that represents the list to which @code{v} is
bound.  Suppose that we now evaluate

@example kotlin
val w = mystery(v)
@end example

Draw box-and-pointer diagrams that show the structures @code{v} and @code{w}
after evaluating this expression.  What would be printed as the values of
@code{v} and @code{w}?
@end quotation

@subsubheading Sharing and identity

We mentioned in @ref{3.1.3} the theoretical issues of ``sameness'' and
``change'' raised by the introduction of assignment.  These issues arise in
practice when individual pairs are @newterm{shared} among different data
objects.  For example, consider the structure formed by

@example kotlin
val x = vlist(VSym("a"), VSym("b")) as VPair
val z1 = cons(x, x)
@end example

@noindent
As shown in @ref{Figure 3.16}, @code{z1} is a pair whose @code{car} and
@code{cdr} both point to the same pair @code{x}.  This sharing of @code{x} by
the @code{car} and @code{cdr} of @code{z1} is a consequence of the
straightforward way in which @code{cons} is implemented.  In general, using
@code{cons} to construct lists will result in an interlinked structure of pairs
in which many individual pairs are shared by many different structures.

