@noindent
In this structure, the pairs in the two @code{(a b)} lists are distinct,
although the actual symbols are shared.@footnote{The two pairs are distinct
because each call to @code{cons} returns a new pair.  The symbols are shared;
a @code{VSym} carries only its name and the language provides no way to mutate
one, so this sharing is undetectable.  Note also that the sharing of pairs is
what enables us to compare them with the identity operator @code{===}, which
simply checks equality of references.}

@noindent
When thought of as a list, @code{z1} and @code{z2} both represent ``the same''
list, @code{((a b) a b)}.  In general, sharing is completely undetectable if we
operate on lists using only @code{cons} and the two selectors.  However,
if we allow mutators on list structure, sharing becomes significant.  As an
example of the difference that sharing can make, consider the following
function, which modifies the @code{car} of the structure to which it is
applied:

@example kotlin
fun setToWow(x: VPair): VPair @{
    (x.car as VPair).setCar(VSym("wow"))
    return x
@}
@end example

@noindent
Even though @code{z1} and @code{z2} are ``the same'' structure, applying
@code{setToWow} to them yields different results.  With @code{z1}, altering
the @code{car} also changes the @code{cdr}, because in @code{z1} the @code{car}
and the @code{cdr} are the same pair.  With @code{z2}, the @code{car} and
@code{cdr} are distinct, so @code{setToWow} modifies only the @code{car}:

@example kotlin
z1
// => ((a b) a b)

setToWow(z1)
// => ((wow b) wow b)

z2
// => ((a b) a b)

setToWow(z2)
// => ((wow b) a b)
@end example

@noindent
One way to detect sharing in list structures is to use the identity operator
@code{===}, the counterpart of the pointer-equality test we introduced in @ref{2.3.1}
as a way to test whether two symbols are equal.  More generally,
@code{x === y} tests whether @code{x} and @code{y} are the same object (that
is, whether they are equal as references).  Thus, with @code{z1} and
@code{z2} as defined in @ref{Figure 3.16} and @ref{Figure 3.17},
@code{z1.car === z1.cdr} is true and @code{z2.car === z2.cdr} is false:

@example kotlin
z1.car === z1.cdr
// => true

z2.car === z2.cdr
// => false
@end example

As will be seen in the following sections, we can exploit sharing to greatly
extend the repertoire of data structures that can be represented by pairs.  On
the other hand, sharing can also be dangerous, since modifications made to
structures will also affect other structures that happen to share the modified
parts.  The mutation operations @code{setCar} and @code{setCdr} should be
used with care; unless we have a good understanding of how our data objects are
shared, mutation can have unanticipated results.@footnote{The subtleties of
dealing with sharing of mutable data objects reflect the underlying issues of
``sameness'' and ``change'' that were raised in @ref{3.1.3}.  We
mentioned there that admitting change to our language requires that a compound
object must have an ``identity'' that is something different from the pieces
from which it is composed.  In the original book's language, we consider this ``identity'' to be the
quality that is tested by pointer equality, and
Kotlin spells the same test @code{===}.  Since in most implementations a
pointer is essentially a memory address, we are ``solving the problem'' of
defining the identity of objects by stipulating that a data object ``itself''
is the information stored in some particular set of memory locations in the
computer.  This suffices for simple programs, but is hardly a general way
to resolve the issue of ``sameness'' in computational models.}

