@subsubheading Mutation is just assignment

When we introduced compound data, we observed in @ref{2.1.3} that pairs
can be represented purely in terms of procedures, with a @code{dispatch}
function that hands back the @code{car} or the @code{cdr} closure for a
message.  The same observation is true for mutable data.  We can implement
mutable data objects as objects closing over assignment and local state.  For
instance, we can extend that representation to handle the two mutators in a
manner analogous to the way we implemented bank accounts using
@code{makeAccount} in @ref{3.1.1}.  Where the book's @code{dispatch} string
selected a closure to call, Kotlin says it directly: the procedural pair is
an object whose selectors read captured @code{var} slots and whose mutators
write them:

@example kotlin
interface PairProc @{
    fun first(): Datum

    fun second(): Datum

    fun setFirst(v: Datum)

    fun setSecond(v: Datum)
@}

fun proceduralPair(
    x: Datum,
    y: Datum,
): PairProc @{
    var firstSlot = x
    var secondSlot = y
    return object : PairProc @{
        override fun first(): Datum = firstSlot

        override fun second(): Datum = secondSlot

        override fun setFirst(v: Datum) @{
            firstSlot = v
        @}

        override fun setSecond(v: Datum) @{
            secondSlot = v
        @}
    @}
@}
@end example

@noindent
Assignment is all that is needed, theoretically, to account for the behavior of
mutable data.  As soon as we admit assignment to our language, we raise all
the issues, not only of assignment, but of mutable data in general.@footnote{On
the other hand, from the viewpoint of implementation, assignment requires us to
modify the environment, which is itself a mutable data structure.  Thus,
assignment and mutation are equipotent: Each can be implemented in terms of the
other.}

@quotation
@strong{@anchor{Exercise 3.20}Exercise 3.20:} @emph{This edition replaces SICP exercise 3.20 with a host-language exercise that keeps its number and teaching objective.}

Suppose we evaluate the following sequence against the
@code{proceduralPair} implementation above:

@example kotlin
val x = proceduralPair(Whole(1L), Whole(2L))
val alias = x

alias.setFirst(Whole(17L))

x.first()
// => Whole(value=17)

val fresh = proceduralPair(Whole(1L), Whole(2L))
fresh.first()
// => Whole(value=1)
@end example

Trace this sequence: which captured @code{var} slot does each call consult,
and why does @code{x.first()} answer @code{Whole(value=17)} after the mutation
went through @code{alias}?  What does the fresh @code{proceduralPair}
answer to the same @code{first()} request, and what does that contrast say
about where the state of a procedural pair lives?  (Compare
@ref{Exercise 3.11}.)
@end quotation
@node	3.3.2, 3.3.3, 3.3.1, 3.3
@subsection Representing Queues

The mutators @code{setCar} and @code{setCdr} enable us to use pairs to
construct data structures that cannot be built with @code{cons}, @code{car},
and @code{cdr} alone.  This section shows how to use pairs to represent a data
structure called a queue.  Section @ref{3.3.3} will show how to represent data
structures called tables.

A @newterm{queue} is a sequence in which items are inserted at one end (called
the @newterm{rear} of the queue) and deleted from the other end (the
@newterm{front}).  @ref{Figure 3.18} shows an initially empty queue in which
the items @code{a} and @code{b} are inserted.  Then @code{a} is removed,
@code{c} and @code{d} are inserted, and @code{b} is removed.  Because items are
always removed in the order in which they are inserted, a queue is sometimes
called a @newterm{FIFO} (first in, first out) buffer.

@float
@anchor{Figure 3.18}
@ifinfo
@quotation
@strong{Figure 3.18:} Queue operations.

@example
Operation                Resulting Queue
val q = Queue()
q.insert(VSym("a"))      a
q.insert(VSym("b"))      a b
q.delete()               b
q.insert(VSym("c"))      b c
q.insert(VSym("d"))      b c d
q.delete()               c d
@end example
@end quotation
@end ifinfo
@ifhtml
@image{../../text/original/figures/chap3/Fig3.18,,119mm,Figure 3.18: Queue operations.,.std.svg}
@end ifhtml
@iftex
@image{../../text/original/figures/pdf/chap3/Fig3.18.std,147mm,,,.pdf}

@end iftex
@caption{@strong{Figure 3.18:} Queue operations.}
@end float

@noindent
In terms of data abstraction, we can regard a queue as defined by the following
set of operations:

@itemize @bullet

@item
a constructor: @code{Queue()} returns an empty queue (a queue containing
no items); the constructor call is all the book's @code{make-queue} needs
here.

@item
two selectors:

@example kotlin
⟨queue⟩.emptyQueue()
@end example

@noindent
tests if the queue is empty.

@example kotlin
⟨queue⟩.frontCell()?.car
@end example

@noindent
returns the object at the front of the queue, or null if the queue is empty;
it does not modify the queue.

@item
two mutators:

@example kotlin
⟨queue⟩.insert(⟨item⟩)
@end example

@noindent
inserts the item at the rear of the queue; where the original handed back the
modified queue, a Kotlin mutator returns @code{Unit}, the book's unspecified
value.

@example kotlin
⟨queue⟩.delete()
@end example

@noindent
removes the item at the front of the queue and returns @code{Either.Right} of
the removed item, or @code{Either.Left(QueueError.EmptyQueue)} if the queue is
empty before the deletion.

@end itemize

@noindent
Because a queue is a sequence of items, we could certainly represent it as an
ordinary list; the front of the queue would be the @code{car} of the list,
inserting an item in the queue would amount to appending a new element at the
end of the list, and deleting an item from the queue would just be taking the
@code{cdr} of the list.  However, this representation is inefficient, because
in order to insert an item we must scan the list until we reach the end.  Since
the only method we have for scanning a list is by successive @code{cdr}
operations, this scanning requires @math{{\Theta(n)}} steps for a list of @math{n}
items.  A simple modification to the list representation overcomes this
disadvantage by allowing the queue operations to be implemented so that they
require @math{{\Theta(1)}} steps; that is, so that the number of steps needed is
independent of the length of the queue.

The difficulty with the list representation arises from the need to scan to
find the end of the list.  The reason we need to scan is that, although the
standard way of representing a list as a chain of pairs readily provides us
with a pointer to the beginning of the list, it gives us no easily accessible
pointer to the end.  The modification that avoids the drawback is to represent
the queue as a list, together with an additional pointer that indicates the
final pair in the list.  That way, when we go to insert an item, we can consult
the rear pointer and so avoid scanning the list.

@noindent
A queue is represented, then, by a pair of pointers, @code{front} and
@code{rear}, which indicate, respectively, the first and last pairs in an
ordinary list.  Since we would like the queue to be an identifiable object, we
gather the two pointers into one object: where the original edition combined
the two pointers with @code{cons} and passed that pair around, this edition
declares a @code{Queue} class whose two @code{var} fields are the pointers,
and the queue itself is the class instance.  @ref{Figure 3.19} illustrates this
representation.

@float
@anchor{Figure 3.19}
@ifinfo
@quotation
@strong{Figure 3.19:} Implementation of a queue as a list with front and rear pointers.

@example
       +---+---+
  q -->| * | *-+-------------------+
       +-|-+---+                   |
         |                         |
         | front-ptr               | rear-ptr
         V                         V
     +---+---+    +---+---+    +---+---+
     | * | *-+--->| * | *-+--->| * | / |
     +-|-+---+    +-|-+---+    +-|-+---+
       V            V            V
     +---+        +---+        +---+
     | a |        | b |        | c |
     +---+        +---+        +---+
@end example
@end quotation
@end ifinfo
@ifhtml
@image{../../text/original/figures/chap3/Fig3.19b,,118mm,Figure 3.19: Implementation of a queue as a list with front and rear pointers.,.std.svg}
@end ifhtml
@iftex
@image{../../text/original/figures/pdf/chap3/Fig3.19b.std,147mm,,,.pdf}

@end iftex
@caption{@strong{Figure 3.19:} Implementation of a queue as a list with front and rear pointers.}
@end float

@noindent
To define the queue operations we begin from the representation, which keeps
the front and rear pointers in two fields, so that every operation can select
and modify them directly:

@example kotlin
class Queue @{
    private var front: VPair? = null
    private var rear: VPair? = null

    fun frontCell(): VPair? = front

    fun rearCell(): VPair? = rear
@end example

@noindent
The original's four procedures --- @code{front-ptr}, @code{rear-ptr},
@code{set-front-ptr!}, and @code{set-rear-ptr!} --- collapse into the two
field accesses themselves; the two readers exist so that exercise 3.21 can
observe the hidden pair, which the fields alone would not show.  We will
consider a queue to be empty if its front pointer is null:

@example kotlin
    fun emptyQueue(): Boolean = front == null
@end example

@noindent
The @code{make-queue} constructor returns, as an initially empty queue, a
queue whose two pointers are both null; the constructor call itself is the
book's name for that, and it gives us the @code{q} the figures label:

@example kotlin
val q = Queue()
@end example

@noindent
To select the item at the front of the queue, we return the @code{car} of the
pair indicated by the front pointer, with null where the original signaled an
error:

@example kotlin
q.frontCell()?.car
@end example

@noindent
To insert an item in a queue, we follow the method whose result is indicated in
@ref{Figure 3.20}.  We first create a new pair whose @code{car} is the item to
be inserted and whose @code{cdr} is the empty list.  If the queue was initially
empty, we set the front and rear pointers of the queue to this new pair.
Otherwise, we modify the final pair in the queue to point to the new pair, and
also set the rear pointer to the new pair.

@float
@anchor{Figure 3.20}
@ifinfo
@strong{Figure 3.20:} Result of using @code{(insert-queue! q 'd)} on the queue of @ref{Figure 3.19}.

@example
       +---+---+
  q -->| * | *-+--------------------------------+
       +-|-+---+                                |
         |                                      |
         | front-ptr                            | rear-ptr
         V                                      V
     +---+---+    +---+---+    +---+---+    +---+---+
     | * | *-+--->| * | *-+--->| * | *-+--->| * | / |
     +-|-+---+    +-|-+---+    +-|-+---+    +-|-+---+
       V            V            V            V
     +---+        +---+        +---+        +---+
     | a |        | b |        | c |        | d |
     +---+        +---+        +---+        +---+
@end example
@end ifinfo
@ifhtml
@image{../../text/original/figures/chap3/Fig3.20c,,141mm,Figure 3.20: Result of using (insert-queue! q 'd) on the queue of Figure 3.19.,.std.svg}
@end ifhtml
@iftex
@image{../../text/original/figures/pdf/chap3/Fig3.20c.std,147mm,,,.pdf}

@end iftex
@caption{@strong{Figure 3.20:} Result of using @code{(insert-queue! q 'd)} on the queue of @ref{Figure 3.19}.}
@end float

@example kotlin
    fun insert(item: Value) @{
        val cell = VPair(item, VNil)
        val tail = rear
        if (tail == null) @{
            front = cell
            rear = cell
        @} else @{
            tail.setCdr(cell)
            rear = cell
        @}
    @}
@end example

@noindent
To delete the item at the front of the queue, we merely modify the front
pointer so that it now points at the second item in the queue, which can be
found by following the @code{cdr} pointer of the first item (see
@ref{Figure 3.21}):@footnote{If the first item is the final item in the queue, the front
pointer will be the empty list after the deletion, which will mark the queue as
empty; we needn't worry about updating the rear pointer, which will still point
to the deleted item, because @code{empty-queue?} looks only at the front
pointer.}

@example kotlin
    fun delete(): Either<QueueError, Value> @{
        val head = front
        return if (head == null) @{
            Either.Left(QueueError.EmptyQueue)
        @} else @{
            front = head.cdr as? VPair
            if (front == null) @{
                rear = null
            @}
            Either.Right(head.car)
        @}
    @}
@end example

@noindent
Where the original signaled an error for the empty queue, @code{delete}
returns @code{Either.Left(QueueError.EmptyQueue)}, the typed shape this
edition gives the book's errors.  And where the footnote lets the original
leave the rear pointer resting on the deleted cell, this @code{delete} also
clears @code{rear} when the last item leaves, so the cells of exercise 3.21
never show a stale pointer.  One operation of the book's queue has no
counterpart in the original section: @code{printQueue}, the print procedure
exercise 3.21 asks Ben for, ships with the class from the start and closes the
definition of it:

@example kotlin
    fun printQueue(): String = front?.toString() ?: "()"
@}
@end example

@float
@anchor{Figure 3.21}
@ifinfo
@strong{Figure 3.21:} Result of using @code{(delete-queue!  q)} on the queue of @ref{Figure 3.20}.

@example
       +---+---+
  q -->| * | *-+--------------------------------+
       +-|-+---+                                |
         +------------+                         |
            front-ptr |                         | rear-ptr
                      V                         V
     +---+---+    +---+---+    +---+---+    +---+---+
     | * | *-+--->| * | *-+--->| * | *-+--->| * | / |
     +-|-+---+    +-|-+---+    +-|-+---+    +-|-+---+
       V            V            V            V
     +---+        +---+        +---+        +---+
     | a |        | b |        | c |        | d |
     +---+        +---+        +---+        +---+
@end example
@end ifinfo
@ifhtml
@image{../../text/original/figures/chap3/Fig3.21c,,141mm,Figure 3.21: Result of using (delete-queue! q) on the queue of Figure 3.20.,.std.svg}
@end ifhtml
@iftex
@image{../../text/original/figures/pdf/chap3/Fig3.21c.std,147mm,,,.pdf}

@end iftex
@caption{@strong{Figure 3.21:} Result of using @code{(delete-queue!  q)} on the queue of @ref{Figure 3.20}.}
@end float

@noindent
The interactions of @ref{Figure 3.18}, against this class:

@example kotlin
val q = Queue()

q.insert(VSym("a"))
q.printQueue()
// (a)

q.insert(VSym("b"))
q.printQueue()
// (a b)

q.delete()
// => Either.Right(a)
q.printQueue()
// (b)

q.insert(VSym("c"))
q.insert(VSym("d"))
q.printQueue()
// (b c d)

q.delete()
// => Either.Right(b)
q.printQueue()
// (c d)
@end example

@noindent
In everyday Kotlin this queue is @code{ArrayDeque}, the standard library's
double-ended queue, which gives the same @math{{\Theta(1)}} ends over an array
rather than over pairs.  The pair-built queue is the one this book uses,
because its insides are the subject.

@quotation
@strong{@anchor{Exercise 3.21}Exercise 3.21:} Ben Bitdiddle decides to test the
queue implementation described above.  He runs a session, printing the queue
with the class's own printer and then inspecting the two pointer cells behind
the representation:

@example kotlin
val q1 = Queue()

q1.insert(VSym("a"))
q1.insert(VSym("b"))
q1.insert(VSym("c"))
q1.insert(VSym("d"))

q1.printQueue()
// (a b c d)

q1.frontCell()
// => (a b c d)

q1.rearCell()
// => (d)
@end example

``It's all wrong!''  he complains.  ``The item @code{d} is in the queue twice:
once in the printed queue and again as the rear pointer.  And the rear pointer
is not a list of the items the way the front one is.''  Eva Lu Ator suggests
that Ben has misunderstood what is happening.  ``It's not that the items are
going into the queue twice,'' she explains.  ``The queue is one chain of
mutable pairs; the front cell is the first pair of that chain, and the rear
cell is the last pair of the very same chain.''  The original Lisp
interpreter, which held the two pointers in a pair of its own and printed
that pair with the standard list printer, showed Ben's equivalent session
there as

@example
(insert-queue! q1 'a)   ((a) a)
(insert-queue! q1 'b)   ((a b) b)
(delete-queue! q1)      ((b) b)
(delete-queue! q1)      (() b)
@end example

@noindent
--- the front chain, then the rear chain in tail position, where a one-pair
chain shows as its single item; and the @code{b} on the last line is the stale
rear pointer, untouched because the original's deletion moved only the front
pointer and its emptiness test looked only at the front pointer too.  Explain
what Eva Lu is talking about.  In particular, show why the views above come
out the way they do: why @code{printQueue} agrees with the front cell, why the
rear cell shows only @code{(d)}, and why the pair of the two pointers is the
same queue as the printed one.  Then write the original printer yourself: a
function that takes a queue and renders the pair of its two pointer cells the
way the standard list printer would, so that Ben's queue renders as
@code{((a b c d) d)} and a queue of @code{a} alone as @code{((a) a)}.
@end quotation

@quotation
@strong{@anchor{Exercise 3.22}Exercise 3.22:} Instead of representing a queue
as a class whose fields hold the two pointers, we can build a queue as an
object whose state is captured by the very function that constructs it.  The
local state will consist of pointers to the beginning and the end of an
ordinary list.  The messages are the queue operations themselves:

@example kotlin
interface MessagePassingQueue @{
    fun emptyQueue(): Boolean

    fun insert(item: Datum)

    fun delete(): Either<QueueError, Datum>

    fun items(): List<Datum>
@}
@end example

@noindent
Thus, the @code{makeQueue} function will have the form

@example kotlin
fun makeQueue(): MessagePassingQueue @{
    var front: PairCell? = ⟨…⟩
    var rear: PairCell? = ⟨…⟩
    ⟨the queue operations,
        as members of the object expression⟩
    return ⟨the object expression⟩
@}
@end example

Complete the definition of @code{makeQueue} and provide implementations of the
queue operations using this representation. The @code{items} operation returns
the contents as a host @code{List<Datum>} rather than printing the queue.
@end quotation

@quotation
@strong{@anchor{Exercise 3.23}Exercise 3.23:} A @newterm{deque} (``double-ended
                                                                 queue'') is a sequence in which items can be inserted and deleted at either the
front or the rear.  Operations on deques are the constructor @code{makeDeque},
the predicate @code{emptyDeque}, selectors @code{frontDeque} and
@code{rearDeque}, and mutators @code{frontInsert},
@code{rearInsert}, @code{frontDelete},
@code{rearDelete}.  Show how to represent deques using pairs, and give
implementations of the operations.@footnote{Be careful not to make the
interpreter try to print a structure that contains cycles.  (See @ref{Exercise 3.13}.)}
All operations should be accomplished in @math{{\Theta(1)}} steps.  In this
edition the cell of the chain is a three-slot mutable pair --- the item, the
previous cell, and the next cell:

@example kotlin
class DPair(
    var item: Value,
    var prev: DPair?,
    var next: DPair?,
)
@end example

@noindent
The deque itself keeps the two end pointers and carries the operations as its
members, exactly like the queue of this section.
@end quotation

@node	3.3.3, 3.3.4, 3.3.2, 3.3
@subsection Representing Tables

When we studied various ways of representing sets in @ref{Chapter 2}, we
mentioned in @ref{2.3.3} the task of maintaining a table of records
indexed by identifying keys.  In the implementation of data-directed
programming in @ref{2.4.3}, we made extensive use of two-dimensional
tables, in which information is stored and retrieved using two keys.  Here we
see how to build tables as mutable list structures.

We first consider a one-dimensional table, in which each value is stored under
a single key.  We implement the table as a list of records, each of which is
implemented as a pair consisting of a key and the associated value. The records
are glued together to form a list by pairs whose @code{car}s point to
successive records.  These gluing pairs are called the @newterm{backbone} of
the table.  In order to have a place that we can change when we add a new
record to the table, we build the table as a @newterm{headed list}.  A headed
list has a special backbone pair at the beginning, which holds a dummy
``record''---in this case the arbitrarily chosen symbol @code{*table*}.
@ref{Figure 3.22} shows the box-and-pointer diagram for the table

@example kotlin
val table = makeTable()

table.insert(VSym("a"), VInt(1))
table.insert(VSym("b"), VInt(2))
table.insert(VSym("c"), VInt(3))

table.lookup(VSym("b"))
// => 2
@end example

@float
@anchor{Figure 3.22}
@ifinfo
@strong{Figure 3.22:} A table represented as a headed list.

@example
+---+---+    +---+---+    +---+---+    +---+---+
| * | *-+--->| * | *-+--->| * | *-+--->| * | / |
+-|-+---+    +-|-+---+    +-|-+---+    +-|-+---+
|            |            |            |
V            V            V            V
+---------+   +---+---+   +---+---+   +---+---+
| *table* |   | * | * |   | * | * |   | * | * |
+---------+   +-|-+-|-+   +-|-+-|-+   +-|-+-|-+
|   |       |   |       |   |
V   V       V   V       V   V
+---+ +---+ +---+ +---+ +---+ +---+
| a | | 1 | | b | | 2 | | c | | 3 |
+---+ +---+ +---+ +---+ +---+ +---+
@end example
@end ifinfo
@ifhtml
@image{../../text/original/figures/chap3/Fig3.22c,,138mm,Figure 3.22: A table represented as a headed list.,.std.svg}
@end ifhtml
@iftex
@image{../../text/original/figures/pdf/chap3/Fig3.22c.std,147mm,,,.pdf}

@end iftex
@caption{@strong{Figure 3.22:} A table represented as a headed list.}
@end float

@noindent
To extract information from a table we use the @code{lookup} operation, which
takes a key as argument and returns the associated value, or null where the
original wrote false.  @code{Lookup} is defined in terms of the
@code{assoc} operation, which expects a key and a list of records as arguments.
Note that @code{assoc} never sees the dummy record.  @code{Assoc} returns the
record that has the given key as its @code{car}.@footnote{Because @code{assoc}
uses structural equality, it can recognize keys that are symbols, numbers, or list
structure.}  @code{Lookup} then checks to see that the resulting record
returned by @code{assoc} is not null, and returns the value (the @code{cdr})
of the record.

The table is a class.  Its first field is the header pair the representation
needs --- a fixed location the operations can mutate through --- and its
constructor carries the table's key test, @code{sameKey}: the default is
@code{equalv}, the original book's structural equality, and exercise 3.24 hands the
constructor a different one.

@example kotlin
class Table(
    val sameKey: (Value, Value) -> Boolean = ::equalv,
) @{
    private val header: VPair = VPair(VSym("*table*"),
        VNil)

    fun assoc(key: Value, records: Value): VPair? @{
        if (records !is VPair) @{
            return null
        @}
        val record = records.car
        return if (record is VPair &&
                sameKey(key,
            record.car)) @{
            record
        @} else @{
            this.assoc(key, records.cdr)
        @}
    @}

    fun lookup(key: Value): Value? = this.assoc(key,
        header.cdr)?.cdr
@end example

@noindent
To insert a value in a table under a specified key, we first use @code{assoc}
to see if there is already a record in the table with this key.  If not, we
form a new record by @code{cons}ing the key with the value, and insert this at
the head of the table's list of records, after the dummy record.  If there
already is a record with this key, we set the @code{cdr} of this record to the
designated new value.  The header of the table provides us with a fixed
location to modify in order to insert the new record.@footnote{Thus, the first
backbone pair is the object that represents the table ``itself''; that is, a
pointer to the table is a pointer to this pair.  This same backbone pair always
starts the table.  If we did not arrange things in this way, @code{insert!}
would have to return a new value for the start of the table when it added a new
record.}

@example kotlin
    fun insert(key: Value, value: Value) @{
        val record = this.assoc(key, header.cdr)
        if (record == null) @{
            header.setCdr(cons(VPair(key,
                    value),
                header.cdr))
            return
        @}
        record.setCdr(value)
    @}
@end example

@noindent
Where the original's @code{insert!} returned the symbol @code{ok}, a Kotlin
mutator returns @code{Unit}, the book's unspecified value; the caller reads
the table's state through @code{lookup}.

@noindent
To construct a new table, we simply create a headed pair whose @code{car} is
the symbol @code{*table*}; the constructor above does it, and the book's
@code{make-table} is the factory that hands one back:

@example kotlin
fun makeTable(): Table = Table()
@end example

@subsubheading Two-dimensional tables

In a two-dimensional table, each value is indexed by two keys.  We can
construct such a table as a one-dimensional table in which each key identifies
a subtable.  @ref{Figure 3.23} shows the box-and-pointer diagram for the table

@example
math:  +: 43    letters:  a: 97
       -: 45              b: 98
       *: 42
@end example

@noindent
which has two subtables.  (The subtables don't need a special header symbol,
since the key that identifies the subtable serves this purpose.)

