@noindent
When we look up an item, we use the first key to identify the correct subtable.
Then we use the second key to identify the record within the subtable:

@example kotlin
    fun lookup2d(key1: Value, key2: Value): Value? @{
        val subtable = this.assoc(key1,
            header.cdr) ?: return null
        return this.assoc(key2, subtable.cdr)?.cdr
    @}
@end example

@noindent
To insert a new item under a pair of keys, we use @code{assoc} to see if there
is a subtable stored under the first key.  If not, we build a new subtable
containing the single record (@code{key-2}, @code{value}) and insert it into
the table under the first key.  The new subtable is itself a headed list,
whose header carries @code{key-1} where the top table carries
@code{*table*} --- the key that identifies the subtable serves the header's
purpose.  If a subtable already exists for the first key, we insert the new
record into this subtable, using the insertion method for one-dimensional
tables described above:

@example kotlin
    fun insert2d(key1: Value,
        key2: Value,
        value: Value) @{
        val subtable = this.assoc(key1, header.cdr)
        if (subtable == null) @{
            header.setCdr(
                cons(VPair(key1,
                                vlist(VPair(key2,
                    value))),
                header.cdr),
            )
            return
        @}
        val record = assoc(key2, subtable.cdr)
        if (record != null) @{
            record.setCdr(value)
            return
        @}
        subtable.setCdr(cons(VPair(key2,
                value),
            subtable.cdr))
    @}
@}
@end example

@noindent
The table of @ref{Figure 3.23} is then:

@example kotlin
val table = makeTable()

table.insert2d(VSym("letters"), VSym("a"), VInt(97))
table.insert2d(VSym("letters"), VSym("b"), VInt(98))
table.insert2d(VSym("math"), VSym("+"), VInt(43))
table.insert2d(VSym("math"), VSym("-"), VInt(45))
table.insert2d(VSym("math"), VSym("*"), VInt(42))

table.lookup2d(VSym("letters"), VSym("b"))
// => 98

table.lookup2d(VSym("math"), VSym("*"))
// => 42

table.lookup2d(VSym("math"), VSym("/"))
// => null
@end example

@subsubheading Creating local tables

The @code{lookup2d} and @code{insert2d} operations defined above take the
table as their receiver.  This enables us to use programs that access more
than one table.  Another way to deal with multiple tables is to have separate
@code{lookup} and @code{insert} operations for each table.  We can do this by
representing a table as an object that maintains an internal table as part of
its local state.  Where the original assembled such a ``table object'' out of
a dispatch procedure that handed back the right operation for a message,
Kotlin already has that object: one call to @code{makeTable()} constructs a
@code{Table} whose records live behind its own header pair, and the
operations are its members.  When a program wants the book's two-name shape,
@code{get} and @code{put} are the operations of one local table, bound once:

@example kotlin
val operationTable = makeTable()

val get = @{ key1: Value,
    key2: Value -> operationTable.lookup2d(key1,
    key2) @}
val put = @{ key1: Value, key2: Value, value: Value ->
    operationTable.insert2d(key1, key2, value)
@}
@end example

@noindent
Using this table object, we could implement the @code{get} and @code{put}
operations used in @ref{2.4.3} for data-directed programming, as
follows: @code{Get} takes as arguments two keys, and @code{put} takes as
arguments two keys and a value.  Both operations access the same local table,
which is encapsulated within the object created by the call to
@code{makeTable}.

@example kotlin
put(VSym("real-part"), VSym("rectangular"), VInt(0))

get(VSym("real-part"), VSym("rectangular"))
// => 0

get(VSym("real-part"), VSym("polar"))
// => null
@end example

@noindent
In everyday Kotlin this table is a @code{MutableMap}: keys in, values out, no
backbone to maintain.  The runtime's own operation table,
@code{sicp.runtime.OpTable}, is this table at runtime scale --- @code{get}
and @code{put} over a map of maps, a later install overwriting an earlier
one, which is the record overwrite of this section's @code{insert} at
hash-map speed.

@quotation
@strong{@anchor{Exercise 3.24}Exercise 3.24:} In the table implementations
above, the keys are tested for equality using @code{equalv}, the runtime's
structural equality, unless the constructor was handed a different @code{sameKey}
predicate.  This is not always the appropriate test.  For instance, we
might have a table with numeric keys in which we don't need an exact match to
the number we're looking up, but only a number within some tolerance of it.
Design a table constructor @code{makeTable} that takes as an argument a
@code{sameKey} predicate that will be used to test ``equality'' of keys.
@code{MakeTable} should return a table whose @code{lookup} and @code{insert}
use that test for a local table.
@end quotation

@quotation
@strong{@anchor{Exercise 3.25}Exercise 3.25:} Generalizing one- and
two-dimensional tables, show how to implement a table in which values are
stored under an arbitrary number of keys and different values may be stored
under different numbers of keys.  The @code{lookup} and @code{insert}
operations should take as input a list of keys used to access the table:

@example kotlin
val t = KeyListTable()

t.insert(listOf(VSym("letters"), VSym("b")), VInt(98))

t.lookup(listOf(VSym("letters"), VSym("b")))
// => 98
@end example
@end quotation

@quotation
@strong{@anchor{Exercise 3.26}Exercise 3.26:} To search a table as implemented
above, one needs to scan through the list of records.  This is basically the
unordered list representation of @ref{2.3.3}.  For large tables, it may
be more efficient to structure the table in a different manner.  Describe,
and in this edition implement, a table where the (key, value) records are
organized as a binary tree, assuming that keys can be ordered in some way
(e.g., numerically or alphabetically).  Define the ordering your table
assumes; the reference solution compares symbols lexicographically by name
and integers numerically.  (Compare @ref{Exercise 2.66} of @ref{Chapter 2}.)
@end quotation

@quotation
@strong{@anchor{Exercise 3.27}Exercise 3.27:} @newterm{Memoization} (also
called @newterm{tabulation}) is a technique that enables a procedure to record,
in a local table, values that have previously been computed.  This technique
can make a vast difference in the performance of a program.  A memoized
procedure maintains a table in which values of previous calls are stored using
as keys the arguments that produced the values.  When the memoized procedure is
asked to compute a value, it first checks the table to see if the value is
already there and, if so, just returns that value.  Otherwise, it computes the
new value in the ordinary way and stores this in the table.  As an example of
memoization, recall from @ref{1.2.2} the exponential process for
computing Fibonacci numbers:

@example kotlin
fun fib(n: Long): Long =
    if (n == 0L) @{
        0L
    @} else if (n == 1L) @{
        1L
    @} else @{
        fib(n - 1) + fib(n - 2)
    @}
@end example

The memoized version of the same procedure is

@example kotlin
val memoFib = makeMemoFib()
@end example

@noindent
where

@example kotlin
fun makeMemoFib(): (Long) -> Long @{
    var memoFib: ((Long) -> Long)? = null
    fun fibStep(n: Long): Long =
        if (n == 0L) @{
            0L
        @} else if (n == 1L) @{
            1L
        @} else @{
            val recurse =
                memoFib
                    ?: error(
                        "memoFib consulted before " +
                            "makeMemoFib finished building it"
                    )
            recurse(n - 1) + recurse(n - 2)
        @}
    val memoized = memoize(::fibStep)
    memoFib = memoized
    return memoized
@}
@end example

@noindent
and the memoizer is defined as

@example kotlin
fun memoize(f: (Long) -> Long): (Long) -> Long @{
    val table = HashMap<Long, Long>()
    return @{ x ->
        val previouslyComputedResult = table[x]
        if (previouslyComputedResult != null) @{
            previouslyComputedResult
        @} else @{
            val result = f(x)
            table[x] = result
            result
        @}
    @}
@}
@end example

@noindent
Two things have changed shape in this edition, and the statement says so.
The book's local table --- the @code{make-table} of this section --- is
stored as a @code{HashMap} keyed by the argument, the host map at
hash-map scale; and where the book asks for an environment diagram, this
edition asks for a trace, because the structure worth watching is the one
table object every recursive level consults, not a nesting of frames.
Trace the computation of @code{memoFib(3)} over this shared table, noting
each consult that answers from the table and each that computes and
stores.  Explain why @code{memoFib} computes the
@math{n^{\hbox{th}}} Fibonacci number in a number of steps proportional to
@math{n}.  Would the scheme still work if we had simply defined
@code{memoFib} to memoize the plain @code{fib}?
@end quotation

@node	3.3.4, 3.3.5, 3.3.3, 3.3
@subsection A Simulator for Digital Circuits

Designing complex digital systems, such as computers, is an important
engineering activity.  Digital systems are constructed by interconnecting
simple elements.  Although the behavior of these individual elements is simple,
networks of them can have very complex behavior.  Computer simulation of
proposed circuit designs is an important tool used by digital systems
engineers.  In this section we design a system for performing digital logic
simulations.  This system typifies a kind of program called an
@newterm{event-driven simulation}, in which actions (``events'') trigger
further events that happen at a later time, which in turn trigger more events,
and so on.

Our computational model of a circuit will be composed of objects that
correspond to the elementary components from which the circuit is constructed.
There are @newterm{wires}, which carry @newterm{digital signals}.  A digital
signal may at any moment have only one of two possible values, 0 and 1.  There
are also various types of digital @newterm{function boxes}, which connect wires
carrying input signals to other output wires.  Such boxes produce output
signals computed from their input signals.  The output signal is delayed by a
time that depends on the type of the function box.  For example, an
@newterm{inverter} is a primitive function box that inverts its input.  If the
input signal to an inverter changes to 0, then one inverter-delay later the
inverter will change its output signal to 1.  If the input signal to an
inverter changes to 1, then one inverter-delay later the inverter will change
its output signal to 0.  We draw an inverter symbolically as in @ref{Figure 3.24}.
An @newterm{and-gate}, also shown in figure 3.24, is a primitive
function box with two inputs and one output.  It drives its output signal to a
value that is the @newterm{logical and} of the inputs.  That is, if both of its
input signals become 1, then one and-gate-delay time later the and-gate will
force its output signal to be 1; otherwise the output will be 0.  An
@newterm{or-gate} is a similar two-input primitive function box that drives its
output signal to a value that is the @newterm{logical or} of the inputs.  That
is, the output will become 1 if at least one of the input signals is 1;
otherwise the output will become 0.

@float
@anchor{Figure 3.24}
@ifinfo
@quotation
