@image{../../text/original/figures/pdf/chap3/Fig3.25c.std,147mm,,,.pdf}

@end iftex
@caption{@strong{Figure 3.25:} A half-adder circuit.}
@end float

@noindent
We will now build a program for modeling the digital logic circuits we wish to
study.  The program will construct computational objects modeling the wires,
which will ``hold'' the signals.  Function boxes will be modeled by functions
that enforce the correct relationships among the signals.

One basic element of our simulation will be the class @code{Wire}, whose
instances are wires.  For example, we can construct six wires as follows:

@example kotlin
val a = Wire()
val b = Wire()
val c = Wire()
val d = Wire()
val e = Wire()
val s = Wire()
@end example

@noindent
We attach a function box to a set of wires by calling the constructor that
builds that kind of box.  The arguments of the constructor are the wires to be
attached to the box.  In this edition the circuit lives inside one
@code{Simulation} object, @code{sim}, which owns the agenda its gates schedule
on.  Given that we can construct and-gates, or-gates, and inverters, we can
wire together the half-adder shown in @ref{Figure 3.25}:

@example kotlin
val sim = Simulation()

sim.orGate(a, b, d)

sim.andGate(a, b, c)

sim.inverter(c, e)

sim.andGate(d, e, s)
@end example

@noindent
The book's interpreter answered @i{ok} to each wiring call; a Kotlin
constructor answers nothing, and the effect is the wiring itself.  Better yet,
we can explicitly name this operation by declaring a member @code{halfAdder}
that constructs this circuit, given the four external wires to be attached to
the half-adder:

@example kotlin
fun halfAdder(
    a: Wire,
    b: Wire,
    s: Wire,
    c: Wire,
) @{
    val d = Wire()
    val e = Wire()
    orGate(a, b, d)
    andGate(a, b, c)
    inverter(c, e)
    andGate(d, e, s)
@}
@end example

@noindent
The advantage of making this definition is that we can use @code{halfAdder}
itself as a building block in creating more complex circuits.  @ref{Figure 3.26},
for example, shows a @newterm{full-adder} composed of two half-adders
and an or-gate.@footnote{A full-adder is a basic circuit element used in adding
two binary numbers.  Here A and B are the bits at corresponding positions in
the two numbers to be added, and @math{\rm C_{in}} is the carry bit from the
addition one place to the right.  The circuit generates SUM, which is the sum
bit in the corresponding position, and @math{\rm C_{out}}, which is the carry
bit to be propagated to the left.} We can construct a full-adder as follows:

@example kotlin
fun fullAdder(
    a: Wire,
    b: Wire,
    cIn: Wire,
    sum: Wire,
    cOut: Wire,
) @{
    val c1 = Wire()
    val c2 = Wire()
    val s = Wire()
    halfAdder(b, cIn, s, c1)
    halfAdder(a, s, sum, c2)
    orGate(c1, c2, cOut)
@}
@end example

