@noindent
Having defined @code{fullAdder} as a function, we can now use it as a
building block for creating still more complex circuits.  (For example, see
@ref{Exercise 3.30}.)

In essence, our simulator provides us with the tools to construct a language of
circuits.  If we adopt the general perspective on languages with which we
approached the study of Lisp in @ref{1.1}, we can say that the
primitive function boxes form the primitive elements of the language, that
wiring boxes together provides a means of combination, and that specifying
wiring patterns as functions serves as a means of abstraction.

@subsubheading Primitive function boxes

The primitive function boxes implement the ``forces'' by which a change in the
signal on one wire influences the signals on other wires.  To build function
boxes, we use the following operations on wires:

@itemize @bullet

@item @code{wire.getSignal()}

@noindent
returns the current value of the signal on the wire.

@item @code{wire.setSignal(⟨@var{new value}⟩)}

@noindent
changes the value of the signal on the wire to the new value and, when the
value differs from the old, runs every action attached to the wire, in the
order the actions were added.

@item @code{wire.addAction(⟨@var{procedure of no arguments}⟩)}

@noindent
asserts that the designated procedure should be run whenever the signal on the
wire changes value, and runs it once immediately, so a newly attached device
learns the signal the wire already carries.  Such
procedures are the vehicles by which changes in the signal value on the wire
are communicated to other wires.

@end itemize

@noindent
In addition, we will make use of the member @code{sim.afterDelay}, which takes
a time delay and a procedure to be run and executes the given procedure after
the given delay.

The book keeps @code{the-agenda} as a global variable and the gate
constructors as procedures over it; this edition gathers them into one
@code{Simulation} class, whose constructor properties are the agenda and a
probe log.  The gate constructors below are members of that class, shown as
the file declares them, and each builds its box on the simulation's own
agenda.  To connect an input to an output through an inverter, we use
@code{addAction} to associate with the input wire a procedure that will be run
whenever the signal on the input wire changes value.  The procedure computes
the @code{logicalNot} of the input signal, and then, after one
@code{inverterDelay}, sets the output signal to be this new value:

@example kotlin
fun inverter(
    input: Wire,
    output: Wire,
) @{
    val invertInput = @{
        val newValue = logicalNot(input.getSignal())
        afterDelay(inverterDelay) @{ output.setSignal(newValue) @}
    @}
    input.addAction(invertInput)
@}

fun logicalNot(s: Long): Long =
    when (s) @{
        0L -> 1L
        1L -> 0L
        else -> throw IllegalArgumentException("Invalid signal: $s")
    @}
@end example

@noindent
@code{logicalNot} is an ordinary file-level function, not a member.  The
book's @code{error} for a signal other than 0 or 1 becomes an exception here,
a guard the wire discipline never lets a well-formed circuit reach.

An and-gate is a little more complex.  The action procedure must be run if
either of the inputs to the gate changes.  It computes the @code{logicalAnd}
(using a function analogous to @code{logicalNot}) of the values of the
signals on the input wires and sets up a change to the new value to occur on
the output wire after one @code{andGateDelay}.

@example kotlin
fun andGate(
    a1: Wire,
    a2: Wire,
    output: Wire,
) @{
    val andAction = @{
        val newValue = logicalAnd(a1.getSignal(),
            a2.getSignal())
        afterDelay(andGateDelay) @{ output.setSignal(newValue) @}
    @}
    a1.addAction(andAction)
    a2.addAction(andAction)
@}
@end example

@quotation
@strong{@anchor{Exercise 3.28}Exercise 3.28:} Define an or-gate as a primitive
function box.  Your @code{orGate} constructor should be similar to
@code{andGate}.
@end quotation

@quotation
@strong{@anchor{Exercise 3.29}Exercise 3.29:} Another way to construct an
or-gate is as a compound digital logic device, built from and-gates and
inverters.  Define a function @code{orGateFromAndGate} that accomplishes
this.  (The book reuses the name @code{or-gate} for the compound device; this
edition already spent that name on exercise 3.28's primitive, so the compound
constructor needs a name of its own.)  What is the delay time of the or-gate
in terms of @code{andGateDelay} and @code{inverterDelay}?
@end quotation

@quotation
@strong{@anchor{Exercise 3.30}Exercise 3.30:} @ref{Figure 3.27} shows a
@newterm{ripple-carry adder} formed by stringing together @math{n} full-adders.
This is the simplest form of parallel adder for adding two @math{n}-bit binary
numbers.  The inputs @math{A_1}, @math{A_2}, @math{A_3}, @dots{}, @math{A_n} and
@math{B_1}, @math{B_2}, @math{B_3},
@dots{}, @math{B_n} are the two binary numbers to be added (each @math{A_k} and
@math{B_k} is a 0 or a 1).  The circuit generates @math{S_1}, @math{S_2},
@math{S_3}, @dots{}, @math{S_n},
the @math{n} bits of the sum, and @math{C}, the carry from the addition.  Write a
function @code{rippleCarryAdder} that generates this circuit.  This edition
fixes the width at four bits, so the constructor takes the fourteen wires of
one 4-bit addition---the @math{A_k} as @code{a1} through @code{a4}, the
@math{B_k} as @code{b1} through @code{b4}, the @math{S_k} as @code{s1} through
@code{s4}---and the carry wires @code{cIn} and @code{cOut}; the carry into
each full-adder is wired exactly as the figure wires it.  The major drawback
of the ripple-carry adder is the need to wait for the carry signals to
propagate.  What is the delay needed to obtain the complete output from an
@math{n}-bit ripple-carry adder, expressed in terms of the delays for
and-gates, or-gates, and inverters?
@end quotation

