@subsubheading Representing wires

A wire in our simulation will be a computational object with two local state
variables: a signal value (initially taken to be 0) and a collection of
action procedures to be run when the signal changes value.  The book
implements the wire, using message-passing style, as a collection of local
procedures together with a @code{dispatch} procedure that selects the
appropriate local operation, just as it did with the simple bank-account
object in @ref{3.1.1}.  Kotlin states the same object directly as a
class, because the dispatch the book writes by hand is the language's own
method dispatch:

@example kotlin
public class Wire @{
    private var signalValue: Long = 0L
    private val actions = mutableListOf<() -> Unit>()

    /** The book's `get-signal`: the current value of the signal. */
    public fun getSignal(): Long = signalValue

    /**
     * The book's `set-signal!`: store the new value and,
         when it differs
     * from the old,
         call every action in the order the actions were
     * added. The loop walks a snapshot so an action may add actions to
     * other wires while it runs.
     */
    public fun setSignal(v: Long) @{
        if (signalValue == v) @{
            return
        @}
        signalValue = v
        for (action in actions.toList()) @{
            action()
        @}
    @}

    /**
     * The book's `add-action!`: register the action and run it once
     * immediately,
         so a device attached to a wire learns the signal the
     * wire already carries (exercise 3.31).
     */
    public fun addAction(action: () -> Unit) @{
        actions.add(action)
        action()
    @}
@}
@end example

@noindent
The member @code{setSignal} tests whether the new signal value changes the
signal on the wire.  If so, it stores the new value and runs each of the action
procedures, in the order they were added.  The book factors that call loop into
a separate procedure @code{call-each}, which calls each of the items in a list
of no-argument procedures; Kotlin's @code{for} loop plays that role directly,
and @code{actions.toList()} takes the snapshot the loop walks, so an action may
add actions to other wires while it runs.

The member @code{addAction} adds the given procedure to the list of procedures
to be run, and then runs the new procedure once.  (See @ref{Exercise 3.31}.)

The book completes its wire object with @code{get-signal}, @code{set-signal!},
and @code{add-action!} as ordinary procedures over the dispatch, so circuits
can be written in procedural style:@footnote{@anchor{Footnote 155} These procedures are simply
syntactic sugar that allow us to use ordinary procedural syntax to access the
local procedures of objects.  It is striking that we can interchange the role
of ``procedures'' and ``data'' in such a simple way.  For example, if we write
@code{(wire 'get-signal)} we think of @code{wire} as a procedure that is called
with the message @code{get-signal} as input.  Alternatively, writing
@code{(get-signal wire)} encourages us to think of @code{wire} as a data object
that is the input to a procedure @code{get-signal}.  The truth of the matter is
that, in a language in which we can deal with procedures as objects, there is
no fundamental difference between ``procedures'' and ``data,'' and we can
choose our syntactic sugar to allow us to program in whatever style we choose.}
Kotlin needs no such sugar: the members of @code{Wire} already @emph{are}
ordinary procedural syntax, and the interchange the footnote celebrates is
exactly what a method call on an object is.

Wires, which have time-varying signals and may be incrementally attached to
devices, are typical of mutable objects.  We have modeled them as a class whose
instances hold local state (the book's captured @code{var}; here, private
properties) modified by assignment.  When a new @code{Wire} is constructed, a
new set of state properties is allocated, and its action list starts empty.

The wires are shared among the various devices that have been connected to
them.  Thus, a change made by an interaction with one device will affect all
the other devices attached to the wire.  The wire communicates the change to
its neighbors by calling the action procedures provided to it when the
connections were established.

@subsubheading The agenda

The only thing needed to complete the simulator is @code{afterDelay}.  The
idea here is that we maintain a data structure, called an @newterm{agenda},
that contains a schedule of things to do.  The following operations are defined
for agendas:

@itemize @bullet

@item
@code{Agenda()} returns a new empty agenda.

@item
@code{agenda.emptyAgenda()} is true if the specified agenda is
empty.

@item
@code{agenda.firstAgendaItem()} returns the first item on the
agenda, as a pair of its time and the action itself, or
@code{Either.Left(AgendaError.EmptyAgenda)} where the book's version raised an
error.

@item
@code{agenda.removeFirstAgendaItem()} modifies the agenda by
removing the first item.

@item
@code{agenda.addToAgenda(⟨@var{time}⟩, ⟨@var{action}⟩)}
modifies the agenda by adding the given action procedure to be run at the
specified time.

@item
@code{agenda.currentTime()} returns the current simulation time.

@end itemize

@noindent
The particular agenda that a circuit runs on belongs to its @code{Simulation}.
The member @code{afterDelay} adds new elements to it:

@example kotlin
fun afterDelay(
    delay: Long,
    action: () -> Unit,
) @{
    agenda.addToAgenda(currentTime + delay.toInt(),
        action)
@}
@end example

@noindent
The simulation is driven by the member @code{propagate}, which operates on
the agenda, executing each procedure on the agenda in sequence.  In general, as
the simulation runs, new items will be added to the agenda, and
@code{propagate} will continue the simulation as long as there are items on the
agenda:

@example kotlin
fun propagate(): Either<AgendaError, Unit> @{
    while (!agenda.emptyAgenda()) @{
        val (time, action) =
            when (val item = agenda.firstAgendaItem()) @{
                is Either.Left -> return Either.Left(item.value)
                is Either.Right -> item.value
            @}
        currentTime = time
        action()
        agenda.removeFirstAgendaItem()
    @}
    return Either.Right(Unit)
@}
@end example

@noindent
The book's @code{propagate} answered @i{done} when the agenda drained; this
edition's answers @code{Either.Right(Unit)}, with the empty-agenda error
carried for a caller who drives the agenda one item at a time.

@subsubheading A sample simulation

The following member, which places a ``probe'' on a wire, shows the
simulator in action.  The probe tells the wire that, whenever its signal
changes value, it should record the new signal value, together with the current
time and a name that identifies the wire:

@example kotlin
fun probe(
    name: String,
    wire: Wire,
) @{
    wire.addAction @{
        probeLog.add(
            "-- $name = $@{wire.getSignal()@}, " +
                "current-time = $currentTime"
        )
    @}
@}
@end example

@noindent
The book's probe printed a line like @code{sum 0  New-value = 0}; this
edition's appends the line @code{-- sum = 0, current-time = 0} to the
simulation's probe log, naming the wire, the new value, and the time.

We begin by initializing the simulation and recalling the delays for the
primitive function boxes:

@example kotlin
public val inverterDelay: Long = 2L
public val andGateDelay: Long = 3L
public val orGateDelay: Long = 5L

val sim = Simulation()
@end example

@noindent
Now we define four wires, placing probes on two of them:

@example kotlin
val input1 = Wire()
val input2 = Wire()
val sum = Wire()
val carry = Wire()

sim.probe("sum", sum)
// -- sum = 0, current-time = 0

sim.probe("carry", carry)
// -- carry = 0, current-time = 0
@end example

@noindent
Each probe printed once at registration, before any signal changed: the
immediate run that exercise 3.31 asks about.  Next we connect the wires
in a half-adder circuit (as in @ref{Figure 3.25}),
set the signal on @code{input1} to 1, and run the simulation:

@example kotlin
sim.halfAdder(input1, input2, sum, carry)

input1.setSignal(1L)

sim.propagate()
// -- sum = 1, current-time = 8
// => Either.Right(Unit)
@end example

@noindent
The @code{sum} signal changes to 1 at time 8.  We are now eight time units from
the beginning of the simulation.  At this point, we can set the signal on
@code{input2} to 1 and allow the values to propagate:

@example kotlin
input2.setSignal(1L)

sim.propagate()
// -- carry = 1, current-time = 11
// -- sum = 0, current-time = 16
// => Either.Right(Unit)
@end example

@noindent
The @code{carry} changes to 1 at time 11 and the @code{sum} changes to 0 at
time 16.

@quotation
@strong{@anchor{Exercise 3.31}Exercise 3.31:} The member @code{addAction} of
the section's @code{Wire} class specifies that when a new action procedure is
added to a wire, the procedure is immediately run.
Explain why this initialization is necessary.  In particular, trace through the
half-adder example in the paragraphs above and say how the system's response
would differ if we had defined @code{addAction} as

@example kotlin
public fun addAction(action: () -> Unit) @{
    actions.add(action)
@}
@end example
@end quotation

@subsubheading Implementing the agenda

Finally, we give details of the agenda data structure, which holds the
procedures that are scheduled for future execution.

The agenda is made up of @newterm{time segments}.  Each time segment is a pair
consisting of a number (the time) and a queue (see @ref{Exercise 3.32}) that
holds the procedures that are scheduled to be run during that time segment.
The book builds the segment as a cons pair whose car is the time and whose cdr
is the queue; this edition states the same pair as a class:

@example kotlin
/** The book's time segment: one timestamp and the queue of actions due
 * at it. */
public class TimeSegment(
    public val time: Int,
    public val queue: ActionQueue,
)
@end example

@noindent
We will operate on the time-segment queues with the same front/rear discipline
the queue of @ref{3.3.2} keeps.  The book could reuse its one queue type
because a list in the original book holds procedures and pairs alike; Kotlin's segment
queues hold no-argument actions, so the simulator carries its own queue with
the same two pointers:

@example kotlin
/**
 * The agenda's segment queue: the front/rear pointer discipline of the
 * 3.3.2 queue,
     typed for the no-argument actions an agenda schedules.
 * `delete` answers null for an empty queue instead of a typed error,
 * because the agenda removes an item only after confirming one exists.
 */
public class ActionQueue @{
    private class Cell(
        val item: () -> Unit,
        var next: Cell?,
    )

    private var front: Cell? = null
    private var rear: Cell? = null

    /** Whether the queue holds no actions. */
    public fun emptyQueue(): Boolean = front == null

    /** The front action,
        still queued; null exactly when empty. */
    public fun frontItem(): (() -> Unit)? = front?.item

    /** The book's `insert-queue!`: hang the new cell off the rear. */
    public fun insert(item: () -> Unit) @{
        val cell = Cell(item, null)
        val tail = rear
        if (tail == null) @{
            front = cell
        @} else @{
            tail.next = cell
        @}
        rear = cell
    @}

    /** The book's `delete-queue!`: advance the front pointer and answer
     * the removed action,
         or null when the queue is empty. */
    public fun delete(): (() -> Unit)? @{
        val head = front ?: return null
        front = head.next
        if (front == null) @{
            rear = null
        @}
        return head.item
    @}
@}
@end example

@noindent
The agenda itself is a one-dimensional table of time segments.  It differs from
the tables described in @ref{3.3.3} in that the segments will be kept
in order of increasing time.  The book scanned and spliced a headed list to
preserve that order; this edition holds the segments in a Kotlin list and
inserts by position.  In addition, we store the @newterm{current time}
(i.e., the time of the last action that was processed) in the simulation that
drives the agenda.  A newly constructed simulation has no time segments and a
current time of 0:@footnote{The book's agenda is a headed list, like the
tables in @ref{3.3.3}, with the current time stored at the head, so it
needed no additional dummy header (such as the @code{*table*} symbol used with
tables).  Kotlin's list carries no header cell either, and the current time
lives one level up, in the @code{Simulation}.}

@example kotlin
/** Where the book's agenda procedures raised,
    this edition answers
 * `Either.Left`. */
public sealed interface AgendaError @{
    public data object EmptyAgenda : AgendaError
@}

/**
 * The book's agenda: the time segments sorted by increasing time,
     one
 * queue of actions per time. The book kept the current time at the head
 * of its agenda list; this edition keeps it in the [Simulation] that
 * drives the agenda,
     where [Simulation.propagate] sets it from each
 * first item before running it.
 */
public class Agenda @{
    private val segments = mutableListOf<TimeSegment>()

    /** The book's `empty-agenda?`. */
    public fun emptyAgenda(): Boolean = segments.isEmpty()

    /** The time of the first segment,
        0 when the agenda is empty. */
    public fun currentTime(): Int = segments.firstOrNull()?.time ?: 0

    /**
     * The book's `add-to-agenda!`: add the action to the queue of its
     * time,
         creating that segment in sorted position when no segment
     * holds the time yet. Actions added to one time queue off behind
     * the actions already waiting there.
     */
    public fun addToAgenda(
        time: Int,
        action: () -> Unit,
    ) @{
        val index = segments.indexOfFirst @{ it.time >= time @}
        if (index >= 0 &&
            segments[index].time == time) @{
            segments[index].queue.insert(action)
            return
        @}
        val segment = TimeSegment(time, ActionQueue())
        segment.queue.insert(action)
        if (index < 0) @{
            segments.add(segment)
        @} else @{
            segments.add(index, segment)
        @}
    @}

    /** The book's `remove-first-agenda-item!`: delete the first action
     * of the first segment,
         and drop the segment when its queue
     * empties. */
    public fun removeFirstAgendaItem() @{
        val first = segments.first()
        first.queue.delete()
        if (first.queue.emptyQueue()) @{
            segments.removeAt(0)
        @}
    @}

    /** The book's `first-agenda-item`: the first segment's time paired
     * with the action at its queue's head; `Either.Left(EmptyAgenda)`
     * where the book raised. */
    public fun firstAgendaItem(): Either<AgendaError,
        Pair<Int,
        () ->
        Unit>> @{
        val first = segments.firstOrNull()
        val item = first?.queue?.frontItem()
        return if (first == null || item == null) @{
            Either.Left(AgendaError.EmptyAgenda)
        @} else @{
            Either.Right(first.time to item)
        @}
    @}
@}
@end example

@noindent
An agenda is empty if it has no time segments; @code{emptyAgenda} is the
book's @code{empty-agenda?}.

To add an action to an agenda, @code{addToAgenda} first checks if the agenda is
empty.  If so, it creates a time segment for the action and installs this in
the agenda.  Otherwise, it scans the segments, examining the time of each.  If
it finds a segment for the appointed time, it adds the action to the associated
queue.  If it reaches a time later than the one to which the action is
appointed, it inserts a new time segment into the agenda just before it.  If it
reaches the end of the segments, it creates a new time segment at the end.

The procedure that removes the first item from the agenda deletes the item at
the front of the queue in the first time segment.  If this deletion makes the
time segment empty, we remove it from the list of segments:@footnote{Observe
that the @code{if} in @code{removeFirstAgendaItem} has no @code{else}
expression.  Such a ``one-armed @code{if} statement'' is used to decide
whether to do something, rather than to select between two expressions.  An
@code{if} without an @code{else} yields @code{Unit} when its predicate is
false, which is exactly the right answer when the only task was conditional.}

The first agenda item is found at the head of the queue in the first time
segment.  Whenever we extract an item, we also update the current
time:@footnote{In this way, the current time will always be the time of the
action most recently processed.  Keeping this time in the simulation, and
refreshing it from each first item before the item runs, ensures that it will
still be available even if the associated time segment has been deleted.}
@code{propagate} takes the pair @code{firstAgendaItem} answers, sets the
current time from it, runs the action, and only then removes it from the
agenda.

@quotation
@strong{@anchor{Exercise 3.32}Exercise 3.32:} The procedures to be run during
each time segment of the agenda are kept in a queue.  Thus, the procedures for
each segment are called in the order in which they were added to the agenda
(first in, first out).  Explain why this order must be used.  In particular,
trace the behavior of an and-gate whose inputs change from 0, 1 to 1, 0 in the
same segment and say how the behavior would differ if we stored a segment's
procedures in an ordinary list, adding and removing procedures only at the
front (last in, first out).
@end quotation
@node	3.3.5, 3.4, 3.2, 3.3
@subsection Propagation of Constraints

Computer programs are traditionally organized as one-directional computations,
which perform operations on prespecified arguments to produce desired outputs.
On the other hand, we often model systems in terms of relations among
quantities.  For example, a mathematical model of a mechanical structure might
include the information that the deflection @math{d} of a metal rod is related to
the force @math{F} on the rod, the length @math{L} of the rod, the cross-sectional
area @math{A}, and the elastic modulus @math{E} via the equation
@displaymath
{dAE} \,=\, {FL.}
@end displaymath
@ifinfo

@example
dAE = FL
@end example

@end ifinfo
Such an equation is not one-directional.  Given any four of the quantities, we
can use it to compute the fifth.  Yet translating the equation into a
traditional computer language would force us to choose one of the quantities to
be computed in terms of the other four.  Thus, a procedure for computing the
area @math{A} could not be used to compute the deflection @math{d}, even though the
computations of @math{A} and @math{d} arise from the same
equation.@footnote{Constraint propagation first appeared in the incredibly
forward-looking @abbr{SKETCHPAD} system of Ivan @ref{Sutherland (1963)}.  A
beautiful constraint-propagation system based on the Smalltalk language was
developed by Alan @ref{Borning (1977)} at Xerox Palo Alto Research Center.  Sussman,
Stallman, and Steele applied constraint propagation to electrical circuit
analysis (@ref{Sussman and Stallman 1975}; @ref{Sussman and Steele 1980}). TK!Solver
(@ref{Konopasek and Jayaraman 1984}) is an extensive modeling environment based on
constraints.}

In this section, we sketch the design of a language that enables us to work in
terms of relations themselves.  The primitive elements of the language are
@newterm{primitive constraints}, which state that certain relations hold
between quantities.  For example, @code{adder(a, b, c)} specifies that the
quantities @math{a}, @math{b}, and @math{c} must be related by the equation
@math{{a + b = c}}, @code{multiplier(x, y, z)} expresses the constraint
@math{{xy = z}}, and @code{constant(32L, x)} says that the value of @math{x}
must be 32.

Our language provides a means of combining primitive constraints in order to
express more complex relations.  We combine constraints by constructing
@newterm{constraint networks}, in which constraints are joined by
@newterm{connectors}.  A connector is an object that ``holds'' a value that may
participate in one or more constraints.  For example, we know that the
relationship between Fahrenheit and Celsius temperatures is
@displaymath
{9C} \,=\, {5(F - 32).}
@end displaymath
@ifinfo

@example
9C = 5(F - 32)
@end example

@end ifinfo
Such a constraint can be thought of as a network consisting of primitive adder,
multiplier, and constant constraints (@ref{Figure 3.28}).  In the figure, we
see on the left a multiplier box with three terminals, labeled @math{{m1}}, @math{{m2}},
and @math{p}.  These connect the multiplier to the rest of the network as follows:
The @math{{m1}} terminal is linked to a connector @math{C}, which will hold the Celsius
temperature.  The @math{{m2}} terminal is linked to a connector @math{w}, which is also
linked to a constant box that holds 9.  The @math{p} terminal, which the
multiplier box constrains to be the product of @math{{m1}} and @math{{m2}}, is linked to
the @math{p} terminal of another multiplier box, whose @math{{m2}} is connected to a
constant 5 and whose @math{{m1}} is connected to one of the terms in a sum.

@float
@anchor{Figure 3.28}
@ifinfo
@strong{Figure 3.28:} The relation @math{9C = 5(F - 32)} expressed as a constraint network.

@example
       +---------+     +---------+   v   +---------+
C -----+ m1      |  u  |      m1 +-------+ a1      |
       |    *  p +-----+ p  *    |       |    *  s +---- F
    +--+ m2      |     |      m2 +--+ +--+ a2      |
    |  +---------+     +---------+  | |  +---------+
  w |                              x| |y
    |    +-----+        +-----+     | |     +-----+
    +----+  9  |        |  5  +-----+ +-----+  32 |
         +-----+        +-----+             +-----+
@end example
@end ifinfo
@ifhtml
@image{../../text/original/figures/chap3/Fig3.28,,142mm,Figure 3.28: The relation 9C = 5(F - 32) expressed as a constraint network.,.std.svg}
@end ifhtml
@iftex
@image{../../text/original/figures/pdf/chap3/Fig3.28.std,147mm,,,.pdf}

@end iftex
@caption{@strong{Figure 3.28:} The relation @math{{9C = 5(F - 32)}} expressed as a constraint network.}
@end float

@noindent
Computation by such a network proceeds as follows: When a connector is given a
value (by the user or by a constraint box to which it is linked), it awakens
all of its associated constraints (except for the constraint that just awakened
it) to inform them that it has a value.  Each awakened constraint box then
polls its connectors to see if there is enough information to determine a value
for a connector.  If so, the box sets that connector, which then awakens all of
its associated constraints, and so on.  For instance, in conversion between
Celsius and Fahrenheit, @math{w}, @math{x}, and @math{y} are immediately set by the
constant boxes to 9, 5, and 32, respectively.  The connectors awaken the
multipliers and the adder, which determine that there is not enough information
to proceed.  If the user (or some other part of the network) sets @math{C} to a
value (say 25), the leftmost multiplier will be awakened, and it will set @math{u}
to @math{{25 \cdot 9 = 225}}.  Then @math{u} awakens the second multiplier, which sets @math{v} to
45, and @math{v} awakens the adder, which sets @math{f} to 77.

@subsubheading Using the constraint system

To use the constraint system to carry out the temperature computation outlined
above, we first create two connectors, @code{c} and @code{f}, by calling the
constructor @code{Connector()}, and link @code{c} and @code{f} in an
appropriate network:

@example kotlin
val c = Connector()
val f = Connector()
celsiusFahrenheitConverter(c, f)
@end example

@noindent
The function that creates the network is defined as follows:

@example kotlin
fun celsiusFahrenheitConverter(c: Connector,
    f: Connector) @{
    val u = Connector()
    val v = Connector()
    val w = Connector()
    val x = Connector()
    val y = Connector()
    multiplier(c, w, u)
    multiplier(v, x, u)
    adder(v, y, f)
    constant(9L, w)
    constant(5L, x)
    constant(32L, y)
@}
@end example

@noindent
This function creates the internal connectors @code{u}, @code{v}, @code{w},
@code{x}, and @code{y}, and links them as shown in @ref{Figure 3.28} using the
primitive constraint constructors @code{adder}, @code{multiplier}, and
@code{constant}.  Each constructor call wires the new constraint into its
connectors' lists of participants and returns the constraint it installed.
Just as with the digital-circuit simulator of @ref{3.3.4},
expressing these combinations of primitive elements in terms of functions
automatically provides our language with a means of abstraction for compound
objects.

To watch the network in action, we can place probes on the connectors
@code{c} and @code{f}, using a @code{probe} function similar to the one we
used to monitor wires in @ref{3.3.4}.  Placing a probe on a connector will
cause a line to be appended to a shared log whenever the connector is given a
value:

@example kotlin
val log = mutableListOf<String>()
probe("Celsius temp", c, log)
probe("Fahrenheit temp", f, log)
@end example

@noindent
Next we set the value of @code{c} to 25.  (The second argument to
@code{setValue} tells @code{c} that this directive comes from @code{User},
the book's @code{user}.)

@example kotlin
c.setValue(25L, User)
// Probe: Celsius temp = 25
// Probe: Fahrenheit temp = 77
@end example

@noindent
The probe on @code{c} awakens and reports the value.  @code{c} also propagates
its value through the network as described above.  This sets @code{f} to 77,
which is reported by the probe on @code{f}.

Now we can try to set @code{f} to a new value, say 212:

@example kotlin
f.setValue(212L, User)
// => Either.Left(Contradiction(old=77, attempted=212))
@end example

@noindent
The connector complains that it has sensed a contradiction: Its value is 77,
and someone is trying to set it to 212.  Unlike the book's @code{error} call,
the refusal comes back as a value, @code{Either.Left} of the
@code{ConstraintError.Contradiction} record, and the network is unchanged.  If
we really want to reuse the network with new values, we can tell @code{c} to
forget its old value:

@example kotlin
c.forgetValue(User)
// Probe: Celsius temp = ?
// Probe: Fahrenheit temp = ?
@end example

@noindent
@code{c} finds that @code{User}, which set its value originally, is now
retracting that value, so @code{c} agrees to lose its value, as shown by the
probe, and informs the rest of the network of this fact.  This information
eventually propagates to @code{f}, which now finds that it has no reason for
continuing to believe that its own value is 77.  Thus, @code{f} also gives up
its value, as shown by the probe.

Now that @code{f} has no value, we are free to set it to 212:

@example kotlin
f.setValue(212L, User)
// Probe: Fahrenheit temp = 212
// Probe: Celsius temp = 100
@end example

@noindent
This new value, when propagated through the network, forces @code{c} to have a
value of 100, and this is registered by the probe on @code{c}.  Notice that the
very same network is being used to compute @code{c} given @code{f} and to
compute @code{f} given @code{c}.  This nondirectionality of computation is the
distinguishing feature of constraint-based systems.

@subsubheading Implementing the constraint system

The constraint system is implemented via objects with local state, in a manner
very similar to the digital-circuit simulator of @ref{3.3.4}.  Here each
object is an ordinary Kotlin object holding its state in @code{private var}
fields, and the book's @code{me} dispatch becomes the object's own methods.
Although the primitive objects of the constraint system are somewhat more
complex, the overall system is simpler, since there is no concern about
agendas and logic delays.

The basic operations on connectors are the following:

@itemize @bullet

@item
@code{⟨@var{connector}⟩.hasValue()} tells whether the connector has a value.

@item
@code{⟨@var{connector}⟩.value()} returns the connector's current value.

@item
@code{⟨@var{connector}⟩.setValue(⟨@var{new-value}⟩, ⟨@var{informant}⟩)}
indicates that the informant is requesting the connector to set its value to
the new value.  The request comes back as @code{Either.Right(Unit)} when the
connector accepts it (or already holds exactly that value) and as
@code{Either.Left(ConstraintError.Contradiction)} when the connector holds a
different one.

@item
@code{⟨@var{connector}⟩.forgetValue(⟨@var{retractor}⟩)} tells the connector
that the retractor is requesting it to forget its value.

@item
@code{⟨@var{connector}⟩.connect(⟨@var{new-constraint}⟩)} tells the connector
to participate in the new constraint.

@end itemize

@noindent
The connectors communicate with the constraints by means of the
@code{Constraint} interface: calling @code{newValue()} on a constraint tells
it that the connector has a value, and calling @code{forgetValue()} tells it
that the connector has lost its value.

@example kotlin
interface Constraint @{
    fun newValue()

    fun forgetValue()
@}
@end example

@noindent
@code{Adder} constructs an adder constraint among summand connectors
@code{a1} and @code{a2} and a @code{sum} connector.  An adder is implemented
as a private class whose local state is the three connectors; the class itself
is this edition's version of the book's @code{me} object, and the constructor
connects the new adder to the designated connectors and returns the constraint
it installed:

@example kotlin
fun adder(a1: Connector,
    a2: Connector,
    sum: Connector): Constraint @{
    val me = Adder(a1, a2, sum)
    a1.connect(me)
    a2.connect(me)
    sum.connect(me)
    return me
@}

private class Adder(
    private val a1: Connector,
    private val a2: Connector,
    private val sum: Connector,
) : Constraint @{
    override fun newValue() @{
        val x = a1.value()
        val y = a2.value()
        val total = sum.value()
        when @{
            x != null &&
                y != null -> sum.setValue(x + y,
                this)
            x != null &&
                total != null -> a2.setValue(total - x,
                this)
            y != null &&
                total != null -> a1.setValue(total - y,
                this)
        @}
    @}

    override fun forgetValue() @{
        sum.forgetValue(this)
        a1.forgetValue(this)
        a2.forgetValue(this)
        newValue()
    @}
@}
@end example

@noindent
In the book, the adder's @code{me} procedure acts as a dispatch to the local
procedures, and two ``syntax interfaces'' (see @ref{Footnote 155} in
@ref{3.3.4}) are used in conjunction with the dispatch; here the
@code{Constraint} interface folds the dispatch and its interfaces into the two
methods a connector calls directly.  Since @code{value()} reads as
@code{Long?}, @code{newValue} reads all three connectors first and each branch
of the @code{when} guards on the two it needs.

The adder's local procedure @code{newValue} is called when the adder is
informed that one of its connectors has a value. The adder first checks to
see if both @code{a1} and @code{a2} have values. If so, it tells @code{sum} to
set its value to the sum of the two addends.  The @code{setter} argument to
@code{setValue} is @code{this}, which is the adder object itself.  If
@code{a1} and @code{a2} do not both have values, then the adder checks to see
if perhaps @code{a1} and @code{sum} have values.  If so, it sets @code{a2} to
the difference of these two.  Finally, if @code{a2} and @code{sum} have
values, this gives the adder enough information to set @code{a1}.  If the
adder is told that one of its connectors has lost a value, it requests that
all of its connectors now lose their values.  (Only those values that were set
by this adder are actually lost.)  Then it runs @code{newValue}.  The reason
for this last step is that one or more connectors may still have a value (that
is, a connector may have had a value that was not originally set by the
adder), and these values may need to be propagated back through the adder.

A multiplier is very similar to an adder. It will set its @code{product} to 0
if either of the factors is 0, even if the other factor is not known.  Its
backward branches divide, and division on @code{Long} values truncates, so a
network that infers a factor must hand the box a quotient it can divide
exactly.

@example kotlin
fun multiplier(m1: Connector, m2: Connector,
    product: Connector): Constraint @{
    val me = Multiplier(m1, m2, product)
    m1.connect(me)
    m2.connect(me)
    product.connect(me)
    return me
@}

private class Multiplier(
    private val m1: Connector,
    private val m2: Connector,
    private val product: Connector,
) : Constraint @{
    override fun newValue() @{
        val x = m1.value()
        val y = m2.value()
        val total = product.value()
        when @{
            x == 0L || y == 0L -> product.setValue(0L,
                this)
            x != null &&
                y != null -> product.setValue(x * y,
                this)
            total != null &&
                x != null -> m2.setValue(total / x,
                this)
            total != null &&
                y != null -> m1.setValue(total / y,
                this)
        @}
    @}

    override fun forgetValue() @{
        product.forgetValue(this)
        m1.forgetValue(this)
        m2.forgetValue(this)
        newValue()
    @}
@}
@end example

@noindent
A @code{constant} constructor simply sets the value of the designated
connector, once, at construction.  A well-formed network never sends a message
to a constant box (only the box itself may set its connector), so the
book's error for a stray @code{I-have-a-value} or @code{I-lost-my-value}
message has nothing to answer, and the box's two methods stay empty.

@example kotlin
fun constant(v: Long,
    connector: Connector): Constraint @{
    val box = ConstantBox()
    connector.connect(box)
    connector.setValue(v, box)
    return box
@}

private class ConstantBox : Constraint @{
    override fun newValue() @{@}

    override fun forgetValue() @{@}
@}
@end example

@noindent
Finally, a probe appends a line to a log about the setting or unsetting of the
designated connector:

@example kotlin
fun probe(
    name: String,
    connector: Connector,
    log: MutableList<String> = mutableListOf(),
): Constraint @{
    val me = Probe(name, connector, log)
    connector.connect(me)
    return me
@}

private class Probe(
    private val name: String,
    private val connector: Connector,
    private val log: MutableList<String>,
) : Constraint @{
    override fun newValue() @{
        log.add("Probe: $name = $@{connector.value()@}")
    @}

    override fun forgetValue() @{
        log.add("Probe: $name = ?")
    @}
@}
@end example

@subsubheading Representing connectors

A connector is represented as one object with local state variables
@code{value}, the current value of the connector; @code{informant}, the object
that set the connector's value; and @code{constraints}, the constraints in
which the connector participates.  @code{Connector()} is the book's
@code{make-connector}: a valueless connector with no constraints.  The book's
local procedures become the object's methods:

@example kotlin
class Connector @{
    private var value: Long? = null
    private var informant: Constraint? = null
    private val constraints = mutableListOf<Constraint>()

    fun hasValue(): Boolean = informant != null

    fun value(): Long? = value

    fun setValue(newValue: Long,
        setter: Constraint): Either<ConstraintError,
            Unit> @{
        val current = value
        if (current == null) @{
            value = newValue
            informant = setter
            informExcept(setter, Constraint::newValue)
            return Either.Right(Unit)
        @}
        if (current == newValue) @{
            return Either.Right(Unit)
        @}
        return Either.Left(ConstraintError.Contradiction(current,
            newValue))
    @}

    fun forgetValue(retractor: Constraint) @{
        if (retractor !== informant) @{
            return
        @}
        value = null
        informant = null
        informExcept(retractor, Constraint::forgetValue)
    @}

    fun connect(source: Constraint) @{
        if (source !in constraints) @{
            constraints.add(0, source)
        @}
        if (hasValue()) @{
            source.newValue()
        @}
    @}

    private fun informExcept(
        exception: Constraint,
        inform: (Constraint) -> Unit,
    ) @{
        for (constraint in constraints) @{
            if (constraint !== exception) @{
                inform(constraint)
            @}
        @}
    @}
@}
@end example

@noindent
The connector's local procedure @code{setValue} is called when there is a
request to set the connector's value.  If the connector does not currently
have a value, it will set its value and remember as @code{informant} the
constraint that requested the value to be set.@footnote{The @code{setter}
might not be a constraint.  In our temperature example, we used @code{User},
the book's @code{user}, as the @code{setter}.}  Then the connector will notify
all of its participating constraints except the constraint that requested the
value to be set.  This is accomplished using the following helper, which
applies a designated action to all constraints in the list except a given one.
The test is physical: @code{!==} is the book's @code{not-eq?}, so two
constraints that merely look alike are never confused.

@example kotlin
private fun informExcept(
    exception: Constraint,
    inform: (Constraint) -> Unit,
) @{
    for (constraint in constraints) @{
        if (constraint !== exception) @{
            inform(constraint)
        @}
    @}
@}
@end example

@noindent
If a connector is asked to forget its value, it runs the local procedure
@code{forgetValue}, which first checks to make sure that the request is
coming from the same object that set the value originally.  If so, the
connector drops the stored value along with the informant (keeping the
two in step, so no constraint can later poll a stale number), and informs
its associated constraints about the loss of the value.

The local procedure @code{connect} adds the designated new constraint to the
list of constraints if it is not already in that list (membership, like the
exception test, is object identity). New members join at the front (the
book's @code{cons} onto the list), so the most recently wired constraint is
the first one consulted.  Then, if the connector has a value, it informs the
new constraint of this fact.

The book's @code{me} object plays two roles in the connector: it dispatches to
the local procedures, and it represents the connector as an object, with a
handful of ``syntax interface'' procedures standing in front of the dispatch.
In this edition the five operations above are the connector's public methods,
and the handle itself names the object:

@example kotlin
connector.hasValue()
connector.value()
connector.setValue(newValue, User)
connector.forgetValue(retractor)
connector.connect(newConstraint)
@end example

@quotation
@strong{In this language: mutable data.}

Kotlin carries the section's mutable state in ordinary objects owning
@code{private var} fields (a queue's front and rear pointers, a table's
cells, a wire's signal, a connector's value and constraint list) or in a
@code{var} captured by the closures behind them.  Two names alias exactly
when they reference one object, and @code{===} settles which:

@example kotlin
val u = Connector()
val v = u
(v === u)
// => true
@end example

The book's mechanism still wins when aliasing must be settled between parts
no compiler sees together: sharing is a fact about references, and
@code{===} is the only witness.
@end quotation

@quotation
