@quotation
@strong{@anchor{Exercise 3.37}Exercise 3.37:} The
@code{celsiusFahrenheitConverter} function is cumbersome when compared with a
more expression-oriented style of definition, such as

@example kotlin
fun celsiusFahrenheit(x: Connector): Connector =
    cPlus(cDiv(cMul(cConst(9L),
            x),
        cConst(5L)),
        cConst(32L))

val c = Connector()
val f = celsiusFahrenheit(c)
@end example

@noindent
(The network multiplies @code{x} by 9 before dividing by 5 because values
here are @code{Long}: @code{cDiv(cConst(9L), cConst(5L))} would truncate to 1
before @code{x} ever contributes, and the book's session would come out 57,
not 77.)

Here @code{cPlus}, @code{cMul}, etc. are the ``constraint'' versions of the
arithmetic operations.  For example, @code{cPlus} takes two connectors as
arguments and returns a connector that is related to these by an adder
constraint:

@example kotlin
fun cPlus(x: Connector, y: Connector): Connector @{
    val z = Connector()
    adder(x, y, z)
    return z
@}
@end example

Define analogous functions @code{cMinus}, @code{cMul}, @code{cDiv}, and
@code{cConst} (constant value) that enable us to define compound constraints
as in the converter example above, and check that the book's sessions still
hold through the expression-style network: setting @code{c} to 25 gives
@code{f} 77, and setting a forgotten @code{f} to 212 gives @code{c}
100.@footnote{The expression-oriented format is convenient because it avoids
the need to name the intermediate expressions in a computation.  Our original
formulation of the constraint language is cumbersome in the same way that
many languages are cumbersome when dealing with operations on compound data.
For example, if we wanted to compute the product @math{{(a + b) \cdot (c + d)}},
where the variables represent vectors, we could work in ``imperative style,''
using functions that set the values of designated vector arguments but do not
themselves return vectors as values:

@example kotlin
vSum(a, b, temp1)
vSum(c, d, temp2)
vProd(temp1, temp2, answer)
@end example

Alternatively, we could deal with expressions, using functions that return
vectors as values, and thus avoid explicitly mentioning @code{temp1} and
@code{temp2}:

@example kotlin
val answer = vProd(vSum(a, b), vSum(c, d))
@end example

Since Kotlin functions can return compound objects as values, we can
transform our imperative-style constraint language into an
expression-oriented style as shown in this exercise.  In languages that are
impoverished in handling compound objects, such as Algol, Basic, and Pascal
(unless one explicitly uses Pascal pointer variables), one is usually stuck
with the imperative style when manipulating compound objects.  Given the
advantage of the expression-oriented format, one might ask if there is any
reason to have implemented the system in imperative style, as we did in this
section.  One reason is that the non-expression-oriented constraint language
provides a handle on constraint objects (e.g., the @code{Constraint} that
@code{adder} returns) as well as on connector objects.  This is useful if we
wish to extend the system with new operations that communicate with
constraints directly rather than only indirectly via operations on
connectors.  Although it is easy to implement the expression-oriented style
in terms of the imperative implementation, it is very difficult to do the
converse.}
@end quotation
